A||||6||WisconsinGT||Key ignition issues
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-12-99||10:12 PM||||Hi folks. I am new to the forum. Just picked up my 88 GT and lov'in it. But with any 11 year old car, I have some problems to conquer. One of them dealing with the difficulty of turning the ignition to start the car. It is loose and sometimes I have to fight with it to turn it on and off. Any ideas? Also, the horn does not work. When I depress the center of the steering wheel I hear a faint clicking sound but no horn noise. What do I need to replace? Where is the horn? Thanks
Z||000001||Yellow85||07-13-99||01:40 AM||||Ignition problem? ......according to aomeone`s post, you can ask dealer to make a new key if you give them you car`s vin# this way you don`t have to change your ignition(if ignition hasn`t get any damages by turning).<p>About horn, i guess your horn is dead.<br>Anyway, you can find horn if you look under the front bumper, around under right side of radiator.
Z||000002||Pretender||07-13-99||03:37 AM||||my local dealer made me a ste up for free. i told them i lost them.maybe they felt sorry for me and decided to give them out for free or maybe they are just free to the owners of pontiac cars.
Z||000003||Carrolles||07-13-99||07:21 AM||||The ignition switch is operated by a shaft inside the steering colums.  It is a rack and pinion sort of arrangement and eventually wears out.<br>Write to Ed Parks at the Fiero factory for particulars.    Fierofactory@juno.com<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000004||theogre||07-13-99||02:55 PM||||It might sound like allot, but unless you have the tools to take apart the steering column it is likely esier, and maybe cheaper, to get a use column from a scrap yard. The column is held by 5 bolts/nuts and some plugs. You can swap a column with the same turn signal stalk in about 30 minutes. GM used these columns in a number of cars. You can get the steering wheel swapped at any service center in about 15 minutes. Buying the 2 tools you need to get into the column will run around 40$, then you add the part cost, and time it takes you, figure the whole mess cost around 100$. You can usually find another column for that or a bit more.<p>Dissembling the column is a pain, even with the tool for the lock plate. Then you have to dig out the lock,,,,, which is burried under the turn signal hardware. If the rack is bad you have to dig even more. All the parts are still available, many are in the red "HELP!" blister packs sold thru many US outlets.
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||07-13-99||03:47 PM||||Yep, Ogre is right. I can remove a steering column in under 10 minutes. But when I had to disassemble it to replace a broken turnlight spring, it took me a few days to get the right tools only!  :)<br>
Z||000006||batboy||07-13-99||10:42 PM||||I had a Monte Carlo once that the horn just made some weird clicking noise when you pushed the button. It turned out to be a bad ground where the horn was mounted. I took the horn off, cleaned it up where it mounts with a wire brush and it worked again. By the way, Fieros have two horns, one high tone and one low tone. They are located tucked up in the front nose on both sides of the car, right in front of the front tires. You just about have to jack the car up to get your head under there to see the horns.
